es holding a governor's race this year, and incumbent Jim Pillen is seeking a second term. Every major race rater calls the contest Solid or Likely Republican, and betting markets put Pillen's chances around 90 percent or higher. Nonpartisan likely-voter polls in September show him leading by roughly 8 to 17 points.\n\nDemocrat Lynne Walz, a former state senator, has drawn attention with a campaign that emphasizes crossover appeal, and two Democratic-aligned polls in September showed the race tied or nearly tied. Those surveys use registered-voter samples and partisan sponsors, which tempers their weight. The race still leans clearly toward Pillen, though the polling gap between sponsor types leaves some room for doubt about the true margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0219, "margin": -11.34}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0357, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0267, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0322, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0312, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0585, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0639, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0714, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0694, "margin": -8.5}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.0605, "margin": -9.0}]}